WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.320
How do marking and zig-zagging

2
00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.060
build Turing-machine deciders? Marking turns

3
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.080
repeated scans into a controlled

4
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.300
matching procedure, while finite control

5
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.700
carries the small amount of

6
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.780
information needed between distant positions.

7
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.860
We will count two zeros

8
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.680
per one, then compare the

9
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.900
two sides of a hash

10
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:18.520
separator.

11
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.940
For words with twice as

12
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.800
many zeros as ones, find

13
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.380
the first unmarked one and

14
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.560
mark it. Return left, find

15
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.380
the first unmarked zero, mark

16
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.140
it, then find and mark

17
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.240
a second unmarked zero. If

18
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.280
either zero is unavailable, reject

19
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.020
because this selected one cannot

20
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.820
receive its required pair.

21
00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.480
After matching that pair, return

22
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.720
to the beginning and repeat.

23
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.080
Each complete round removes one

24
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.940
unmarked one and two unmarked

25
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.460
zeros from future consideration. The

26
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:49.580
order of symbols in the

27
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:52.040
input is unrestricted, so restarting

28
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.560
the scan is essential instead

29
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.140
of assuming zeros form an

30
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:55.820
initial block.

31
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.100
When no unmarked one remains,

32
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.180
scan once more for leftover

33
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.420
unmarked zeros. If any remain,

34
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.520
reject; if every input symbol

35
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.300
was matched, enter the accepting

36
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.250
state. Checking only that every

37
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.460
one found a pair would

38
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.260
incorrectly accept a word with

39
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.120
too many zeros.

40
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.980
The empty word has zero

41
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.480
zeros and zero ones, so

42
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.180
it satisfies the equation and

43
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.540
is accepted. Each outer round

44
00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.040
marks a new one, and

45
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.360
every scan is over a

46
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.540
finite used tape segment, proving

47
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:27.680
halt on every input. With

48
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.420
at most linearly many rounds

49
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.020
and linear scans per round,

50
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.360
a straightforward implementation has a

51
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.620
quadratic scan bound.

52
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.080
For the equality-of-counts variant, use

53
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.840
one zero per one instead

54
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.780
of two. For its complement,

55
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.880
reverse accept and reject outcomes

56
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.700
only after establishing that the

57
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.320
original machine always halts. The

58
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.900
same swap would not safely

59
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.660
complement a recognizer that might

60
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:51.980
loop.

61
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.940
Now consider the copying language

62
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.760
w hash w. First validate

63
00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.400
that there is exactly one

64
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.840
separator and that the other

65
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.860
symbols are binary. Mark the

66
00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.380
next unchecked symbol on the

67
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.280
left, remember its value in

68
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.400
finite control, then zig-zag right

69
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.940
to the next unchecked symbol

70
00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.840
on the other side.

71
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.380
The right symbol must match

72
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.060
the remembered bit, and it

73
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.400
is then marked too. Return

74
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.860
to the left and repeat

75
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.220
for the next pair, so

76
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.800
zero one hash zero one

77
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.780
first matches zeros and then

78
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.140
ones. A mismatch, early end

79
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.480
or extra right-side symbol must

80
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:29.900
reject.

81
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.600
When the left side has

82
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.980
no unchecked symbol, verify that

83
00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.520
the right side also has

84
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.700
none. The input consisting only

85
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.800
of hash represents two empty

86
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.680
copies and should be accepted,

87
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.560
while a missing separator is

88
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.100
malformed. Each successful round marks

89
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.620
a fresh pair, giving a

90
00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.420
finite progress measure and a

91
00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:50.320
halting decider.

92
00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.960
Things to take note of.

93
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.920
Describe where scans restart, what

94
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.640
temporary marks mean, what information

95
00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.780
finite control remembers and how

96
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.040
every loop makes progress. Form

97
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.480
checks and final leftovers are

98
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.060
part of the algorithm, not

99
00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.820
optional polish; successful matches alone

100
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:08.960
do not prove the language

101
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:09.840
